prompts.template:
List all tasks in summary form: id, subject, status, owner, blockedBy.
Use it to find available work (pending, unowned, unblocked), check progress, or spot blocked tasks — prefer the lowest ID first, since earlier tasks usually set up context for later ones. A task with a non-empty blockedBy cannot be claimed until its dependencies resolve. Use TaskGet for full detail including comments.
{%- if teamEnabled %}
In a team the list is shared: check it after each task, claim unowned unblocked tasks with TaskUpdate (`owner` = your name), create tasks for new work, mark yours completed when done, and tell the lead if everything is blocked.
{%- endif %}

Execute a skill in this conversation. Skills carry specialized capabilities and domain knowledge; when one matches the request, invoking it is a BLOCKING REQUIREMENT — do it before responding about the task.
- Invoke with the skill name plus optional args: `skill: "pdf"`, `skill: "commit", args: "-m 'Fix bug'"`, `skill: "ms-office-suite:pdf"`.
- A "/something" the user mentions is a skill.
- Never mention a skill without calling it; never re-invoke one that is already running.
- Not for built-in CLI commands (/help, /clear).
- A `<command-name>` tag in the turn means the skill is already loaded — follow it directly.
- A name that appears under <available_deferred_tools> is a deferred tool: use ToolSearch + DeferExecuteTool instead.
<available_skills>
{%- if skillGroups and skillGroups.length > 0 -%}
{%- for group in skillGroups %}
skills under folder: {{ group.folder }}
{%- for skill in group.skills %}
- {{ skill.name }}{% if skill.truncatedDescription == undefined and skill.description %}: {{ skill.description }}{% elif skill.truncatedDescription %}: {{ skill.truncatedDescription }}{% else %};{% endif %}
{%- endfor -%}
{%- endfor -%}
{%- endif %}
{%- if skillsOverview and skillsOverview.omittedCount > 0 %}
Omitted skills: {{ skillsOverview.omittedCount }}.
{%- endif %}
</available_skills>

Launch a subagent for a complex, multi-step task in an isolated context.
Available agent types and their tool access:
<avaliable_subagents>
- general-purpose: handles multi-step research, codebase exploration, and execution. Use when the task needs iterative investigation or substantial autonomous work. (Tools: *)
{%- if agents and agents.length > 0 -%}
{%- for agent in agents -%}
{%- if agent.asTool %}
- {{agent.name}}{%- if agent.truncatedDescription == undefined -%}: {{agent.description}}{%- elif agent.truncatedDescription %}: {{agent.truncatedDescription}}{%- endif -%} (Tools: {%- if agent.tools and agent.tools.length > 0 -%}{{agent.tools.join(',')}}{%- endif -%})
{%- endif -%}
{%- endfor -%}
{%- endif %}
</avaliable_subagents>
- `description`: 3–5 words. `subagent_type` defaults to general-purpose; match the type to the work. Read-only agents (Explore, Plan) get research only, never edits.
- The result returns to you only, never to the user: summarize it yourself and verify actual file changes before reporting work as done.
- Write a self-contained prompt: goal, relevant context, what is already known or ruled out, concrete pointers such as paths and line numbers. Never delegate synthesis with "based on your findings, fix it".
- Start independent agents concurrently in one message; `run_in_background` only when you do not need the result now.
- Reach a running agent with SendMessage (by `name`); continue a finished one with `resume`.
